            "textentryhtml":"&lt;1962-1991 D. Daniel Michel, New York (62:085); 1991 Ancient Art of the New World, New York (broker for above); 1991-present Private US Collection; 11/22/22 Giquello et Associés, Paris, auction, lot 79’ consigned by above\n",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>Ceramic figurines associated with the islands of Jaina and Uaymil, off the west coast of Campeche, Mexico, provide an exceptionally rich record of noble Maya costume and adornment, including variations related to gender and courtly position. Looters disinterred hundreds of such figurines, all presumably associated with burials. The works vary dramatically in terms of detail and mode of manufacture, with some meticulously shaped by hand, others pressed from clay molds, and yet others fabricated through a combination of molded and modeled components. Many of the figurines produce sound, as rattles filled with clay pellets or as whistles, some producing multiple tones. The sound likely served to enliven and spiritually animate the subjects of these works. Although archaeological data is limited, individuals\n\nwere buried with one or a few figurines, but they do not generally accord with the status or gender of the interred.\n</p><p>A rarity among Jaina figurines, this single ceramic sculpture portrays a man and a woman who interact dynamically. A small group of Jaina figural couples portray women engaged in sexually charged interactions with elderly men, clowns, or monkeys. These figurines may depict the\n\ncourtly interactions of courtesans with inebriated elderly men, although they more likely represent ritual performances that subvert social norms. Similar performances, involving lewd interactions between women and elderly men, all staged by masked young men, continue to be performed in Maya communities from Yucatán, Mexico, to the Guatemalan highlands. Although the male here is shown as youthful, the protective wrappings on his head may signal a now-missing mask that would have disguised him as an elderly man or a monkey.\n </p>",
            "remarks":null
        }
    ],
